{% doc %}
  blocks/text.liquid

  Every run of text in the theme, headings included.

  ## Why there is no separate heading block

  There used to be one, and the two files were near-identical: the same
  typography controls, the same colour controls, the same spacing controls, kept
  in step by hand. A merchant who learned one still had to guess which of the two
  a given piece of text was, and the answer was usually "whichever the section
  preset happened to add".

  So the heading block is gone and this block does both jobs. **Heading** and
  **Text** are two `presets` on this one file, which is why both still appear as
  separate entries when adding a block — the merchant's mental model is
  unchanged, there is simply one implementation underneath instead of two.

  ## Preset chooses the tag as well as the look

  `Default` means "leave my markup alone". An `<h2>` typed into the field stays
  an `<h2>` and takes its size from Theme settings › Typography, exactly as it
  would anywhere else on the page — so a merchant can write a small heading and
  a paragraph in one block and have both look right without touching a setting.

  Every other preset is the merchant overriding that. `Heading 1`–`Heading 6`
  restyle *everything* in the field to that level and normalise the tags to
  match, so an `<h1>` inside a block set to Heading 2 comes out at H2 size and
  as an `<h2>` element. Size and outline saying different things is the failure
  mode worth avoiding here: the outline is the half nobody can see, and it is
  the half screen readers and search engines navigate by.

  `Paragraph` and `Subheading` point at the theme's body and small text tokens.
  `Custom` is the escape hatch and opens the full override set.

  ## Presets are classes, overrides are inline properties

  Only the Custom preset and the colour reach Liquid — see
  `snippets/block-typography.liquid` for why the named presets resolve in CSS
  instead. The short version: the theme's type tokens already exist as custom
  properties, and pointing a class at them means Theme settings › Typography
  stays the one place the scale is defined.

  ## Highlight

  Highlight styles paint part of the text, and **Apply to** says which part —
  the italic runs by default, or the bold runs, or the whole block. Marking the
  words with the editor's own buttons keeps them attached to the copy, so a
  rewrite cannot leave a separate "words to highlight" field pointing at words
  that are no longer there.

  Italic being the default is also what made this look broken: with no italics
  in the field there is nothing to paint. `Entire text` needs no markup at all,
  so the style can always be seen working first and narrowed down after.

  ## One style attribute

  Spacing is pulled in as declarations rather than as its own attribute. Two
  `style` attributes on one element is invalid HTML, the browser keeps the first
  and discards the rest, and that is how the padding sliders here used to move a
  value that never reached the page.
{% enddoc %}

{%- liquid
  assign s = block.settings

  assign preset = s.preset | default: 'default'
  assign alignment = s.alignment | default: 'start'
  assign alignment_mobile = s.alignment_mobile | default: 'start'
  assign color_mode = s.color | default: 'inherit'
  assign highlight = s.highlight_style | default: 'none'

  # ---- tag ----------------------------------------------------------------
  # `Default` leaves the merchant's markup exactly as typed — an `<h2>` written
  # into the field stays an `<h2>` and takes its size from the global scale,
  # same as it would anywhere else on the page.
  #
  # Any heading preset is the merchant saying "all of this is a heading at this
  # level", so it normalises the tags to match: paragraphs become that heading,
  # and a heading typed at some other level is moved to it. Otherwise the size
  # would say H2 while the document outline still said H1, and the outline is
  # the half nobody can see.
  assign heading_levels = 'h1 h2 h3 h4 h5 h6'
  assign is_heading = false
  if heading_levels contains preset
    assign is_heading = true
  endif

  assign body = s.text

  if is_heading
    assign open_tag = '<' | append: preset | append: '>'
    assign close_tag = '</' | append: preset | append: '>'
    assign body = body | replace: '<p>', open_tag | replace: '</p>', close_tag

    assign levels = heading_levels | split: ' '
    for level in levels
      unless level == preset
        assign find_open = '<' | append: level | append: '>'
        assign find_close = '</' | append: level | append: '>'
        assign body = body | replace: find_open, open_tag | replace: find_close, close_tag
      endunless
    endfor
  endif

  # ---- width --------------------------------------------------------------
  # Both a `flex` and an `inline-size`, for the same reason the image and group
  # blocks need both: a text block is only a flex item when its parent is a
  # group. At the top level of a section `flex` is inert, so Fit on its own did
  # nothing there and every block came out full width.
  if s.width == 'fit'
    assign width_flex = '0 0 auto'
    assign width_size = 'fit-content'
  else
    assign width_flex = '1 1 0%'
    assign width_size = '100%'
  endif

  if s.width_mobile == 'fit'
    assign width_flex_mobile = '0 0 auto'
    assign width_size_mobile = 'fit-content'
  else
    assign width_flex_mobile = '1 1 0%'
    assign width_size_mobile = '100%'
  endif

  # ---- classes ------------------------------------------------------------
  assign classes = 'block-text rte'
  assign classes = classes | append: ' block-text--' | append: preset
  assign classes = classes | append: ' block-text--align-' | append: alignment
  assign classes = classes | append: ' block-text--align-mobile-' | append: alignment_mobile

  case s.max_width
    when 'narrow'
      assign classes = classes | append: ' block-text--measure-narrow'
    when 'normal'
      assign classes = classes | append: ' block-text--measure-normal'
  endcase

  if color_mode == 'gradient'
    assign classes = classes | append: ' block-text--gradient'
  elsif color_mode != 'inherit' and color_mode != blank
    comment
      Only once the merchant actually picked a colour. The rule this turns on
      hands the colour down to the headings and paragraphs inside the rich text,
      and doing that unconditionally would repaint a heading that was meant to
      keep `--color-heading` from the colour scheme.
    endcomment
    assign classes = classes | append: ' block-text--colored'
  endif

  if highlight != 'none' and highlight != blank
    assign highlight_target = s.highlight_target | default: 'em'
    assign classes = classes | append: ' block-text--highlight block-text--highlight-' | append: highlight
    assign classes = classes | append: ' block-text--hl-' | append: highlight_target
  endif

  if s.background
    assign classes = classes | append: ' block-text--has-background'
  endif

  if s.disable_line_break_mobile
    assign classes = classes | append: ' block-text--no-wrap-mobile'
  endif
-%}

{%- capture block_style -%}
  {%- render 'spacing', block: s, declarations: true -%}
  {%- render 'block-typography', block: s -%}
  --block-text-flex: {{ width_flex }};
  --block-text-width: {{ width_size }};
  --block-text-flex-mobile: {{ width_flex_mobile }};
  --block-text-width-mobile: {{ width_size_mobile }};
  {%- if highlight != 'none' and s.highlight_color != blank -%}
    --block-highlight-color: {{ s.highlight_color }};
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if s.background -%}
    {%- if s.background_color != blank -%}
      --block-text-background: {{ s.background_color }};
    {%- endif -%}
    --block-text-radius: {{ s.border_radius | default: 0 }}px;
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endcapture -%}

{%- if s.text != blank -%}
  <motion-effect
    {% render 'motion-attrs', kind: 'text', block: s %}
    class="{{ classes }}"
    data-block-spacing
    style="{{ block_style | strip_newlines | strip }}"
    {{ block.shopify_attributes }}
  >
    {{ body }}
  </motion-effect>
{%- endif -%}

